\details{
The model of one stratum has six steps:
\enumerate{
\item the death rates of each year give life-table deaths \code{d[x, t]} with
radix 1 at the first age (\code{\link[=life_table]{life_table()}} conventions); for a closed age
range, the survivors at its upper bound are an extra part, so that the
parts sum to 1;
\item \code{a[x]} is the geometric mean of \code{d[x, t]} over the years, closed to 1;
\item the deaths are centred by perturbation (\code{d[x, t] / a[x]}, closed) and
transformed by the centred log-ratio (clr);
\item the first component of a singular value decomposition gives
\verb{clr(d[x, t] / a[x]) = b[x] k[t]} (\code{b} sums to zero);
\item \code{k[t]} follows a random walk with drift (ARIMA(0,1,0) with drift, the
default of the reference implementation);
\item forecasts are transformed back (inverse clr, perturbation by \code{a},
closure) to life-table deaths and then to death rates.
}

With \code{jump_off = "observed"} (the default, as \code{jumpchoice = "actual"} of
the reference implementation), forecasts are the observed deaths of the
last year \code{T} perturbed by the change of the model:
\code{d[x, T + j]} is proportional to \verb{d_obs[x, T] exp(b[x] (k[T + j] - k[T]))}.
With \code{"fitted"}, forecasts start from the fitted deaths of the last year.
The forecast of year \code{T + j} uses the index \code{j} steps ahead; the reference
implementation uses \code{j + 1} steps, which makes no difference for the
observed jump-off with a random walk with drift.

\strong{Coherent model.} With \code{coherent = TRUE} or \code{common_by} (for example
\code{"sex"}), the strata of each group (all strata, or each level of
\code{common_by}) share a common component (Bergeron-Boucher et al. 2017):
\verb{clr(d[x, t, i] / a[x, i]) = B[x] K[t] + b[x, i] k[t, i]}. The common
component \verb{B K} is the first component of the CoDa model of the average
distribution of the group, the closed geometric mean of the life-table
deaths of its strata (the centre of the compositions). The deviations of
each stratum are the first component of the remaining variation. \code{K}
follows a random walk with drift, and \code{k[t, i]} an AR(1) process with mean
(estimated by maximum likelihood), as in the reference implementation
(\code{model.OeppenC()}, defaults \code{order.B = c(0, 1, 0)} with drift and
\code{order.D = c(1, 0, 0)} without drift), so the deviations stay bounded. If
the data carry a hierarchy (see \code{\link[=mt_aggregate_strata]{mt_aggregate_strata()}}), the average uses
the bottom strata only, as \code{\link[=li_lee]{li_lee()}} does.

\strong{Open age interval.} Life-table deaths of an open interval are the
survivors at its lower bound; they do not determine its death rate, and
the reference implementation forecasts the deaths only. Here the log death
rate of the open interval gets loadings on the same indexes, estimated by
least squares: \verb{log m[open, t] = a_o + b_o k[t]}, and in the coherent model
\verb{log m[open, t, i] = a_o[i] + B_o K[t] + b_o[i] k[t, i]}, with \code{B_o} from the
mean log rate of the group. The open interval thus follows the rest of the
age pattern (and the jump-off rule). An auxiliary random walk on the
observed log rate, as in \code{\link[=tucker3]{tucker3()}}, accumulates the noise of the few
deaths at the highest ages: on French data (ages 100+, forecasts of 10
years) a quarter to a half of its paths exceeded a rate of 1.

\strong{Zero deaths.} Cells with zero deaths have no logarithm. As
\code{replace.zeros()} of the reference implementation (method \code{"min"}), zero
rates are replaced by half the smallest positive rate of the stratum in
the training years; a warning is given when more than 10\% of the cells
are affected.

Draws simulate the indexes: the random walk with drift includes the
uncertainty of the drift (normal, with the variance of the mean change),
the AR(1) processes their innovations; \code{a} and \code{b} are fixed. Draws are
expected rates; predictive draws add extra-Poisson and Poisson variation.
The model weights all ages equally on the clr scale, so the noise of ages
with few deaths enters \code{k}; its intervals can be wider than those of
\code{\link[=lee_carter]{lee_carter()}}.
